From d174d19bfa887182accd9dc6475f71f543108fa7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: b1nc0d3x <b1nc0d3x@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 21:51:33 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/7] net80211: add a framework for FullMAC devices

A FullMAC device runs scanning, authentication, association and often
the key handshake in its own firmware, and gives the host Ethernet
frames.  net80211 is built for devices that hand the host 802.11 frames
and leave those steps to it, so every FullMAC driver ends up faking the
same parts: turning firmware scan results into scan entries, keeping
net80211 from walking a state machine the firmware has already
finished, jumping the vap to RUN when the link comes up, turning key
installs into firmware commands and keeping the country in step.

ieee80211_fullmac does that once.  A driver fills in a struct
ieee80211_fullmac_ops, whose four required operations are scan, join,
leave and set a key, calls ieee80211_fmac_attach() after
ieee80211_ifattach(), and reports firmware events through a set of
up-calls.  The optional operations decide what the device offers:
access point mode, power save, signal reports, station aging, a PMK for
the firmware's own supplicant, and external SAE with PMKSA caching.
Everything is built on what net80211 already has: the ic and vap method
pointers, IEEE80211_FEXT_SCAN_OFFLOAD and IEEE80211_ROAMING_DEVICE, the
scan cache, routing socket messages and SIOCS80211 requests.  The
framework's state hangs off a new ic_fmac pointer, which takes one of
the ic_spare slots, so struct ieee80211com keeps its size.

WPA3-SAE on a device that leaves SAE to the host needs a path between
wpa_supplicant and the firmware.  Add three routing socket messages
(RTM_IEEE80211_EXTAUTH, RTM_IEEE80211_MGMT_RX and
RTM_IEEE80211_ASSOC_REJECT) and four requests (IEEE80211_IOC_MGMT_TX,
IEEE80211_IOC_EXTAUTH_STATUS, IEEE80211_IOC_EXTAUTH_CAPS and
IEEE80211_IOC_PMKSA).  The matching wpa_supplicant change goes to the
hostap project separately.

The bwfm(4) driver uses it on BCM43602 (PCIe), BCM43455 (SDIO) and
BCM43236 (USB), for WPA2 and WPA3-SAE stations, roaming, and access
points with 802.11w.  ieee80211_fullmac(9) documents the interface.
